## How Excalidraw cleans up bindings on element deletion

**Where it's triggered:** `Delete`/`Backspace` runs `actionDeleteSelected` (`packages/excalidraw/actions/actionDeleteSelected.tsx`). It marks the selected elements (and their frame children/bound labels) `isDeleted: true`, then calls:

```ts
fixBindingsAfterDeletion(nextElements, nextElements.filter((el) => el.isDeleted));
```

(A second call site exists in `App.tsx` for the case where an empty text element is discarded while editing.)

**The cleanup function** — `fixBindingsAfterDeletion` in `packages/excalidraw/element/binding.ts` — takes the full scene element list plus the just-deleted elements, and for each deleted element runs two complementary passes:

```ts
export const fixBindingsAfterDeletion = (sceneElements, deletedElements) => {
  const elements = arrayToMap(sceneElements);
  for (const element of deletedElements) {
    BoundElement.unbindAffected(elements, element, mutateElement);
    BindableElement.unbindAffected(elements, element, mutateElement);
  }
};
```

Bindings in Excalidraw are bidirectional: a bindable shape keeps a `boundElements` array (listing ids/types of arrows and text bound to it), and a bound element keeps back-references (`containerId`, or an arrow's `startBinding.elementId`/`endBinding.elementId`, or `frameId`). Both directions need fixing depending on which side was deleted:

- **`BoundElement.unbindAffected`** — treats the deleted element as a *bound* element (e.g. a deleted arrow or text label). It walks the bindable elements it pointed to (via `containerId`/`startBinding`/`endBinding`, using `bindableElementsVisitor`) and, for each still-alive one, removes the deleted element's id from that bindable element's `boundElements` array (via `newBoundElements`, which filters out matching ids).

- **`BindableElement.unbindAffected`** — treats the deleted element as a *bindable* container/shape. It walks its `boundElements` (via `boundElementsVisitor`) and, for each still-alive bound element, nulls out the reference back to the deleted element (`containerId: null`, or `startBinding`/`endBinding: null`, depending on which property pointed at it).

Both helpers skip anything already deleted (`if (!x || x.isDeleted) return`), since dangling references between two elements that are both being deleted don't matter and undo/redo can restore them together.

Updates are applied via `mutateElement`, so this happens synchronously as part of the same delete action/undo entry — surviving elements end up with their `boundElements` arrays and `containerId`/`startBinding`/`endBinding` fields scrubbed of any id that no longer exists in the scene.

There's a mirror-image helper, `fixBindingsAfterDuplication`, and `rebindAffected` methods on both classes used when elements are restored (undo) or duplicated, which re-establish these same bidirectional links.
